#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ccrc-api — the closed client for the ccrc coordination API.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. A repo may legitimately deny `Bash(curl:*)` and `Bash(wget:*)`
# in its committed settings, and on 2026-08-26 one did. The worker on that repo
# could not read or ack its mail at all — twelve permission denials in one wave —
# while the coordinator on the same programme kept working, because
# `resp=$(curl …)` happens to slip past that matcher. The deny rule was doing its
# job on one session and being evaded by accident on the other. Neither half of
# that is acceptable, and the fix is not to soften the deny.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT CURL UNDER ANOTHER NAME. A repo denying curl is denying
# ARBITRARY OUTBOUND HTTP BY THE AGENT. This is not that, and the difference is
# mechanical rather than intended:
#
#   * No argument here is a URL, a host, or a path. There is no `--url`, no
#     `--host`, no `--path`, no `--raw`, and deliberately no passthrough verb.
#     Adding one would make this curl with a different name and would
#     retroactively make every install of it an evasion.
#   * The address comes from `~/.ccrc/agent.env`'s `CCRC_SERVER_URL` — config
#     this process's caller does not supply. Absent, it REFUSES; it never guesses
#     a host. (A worker guessed one on 2026-08-25 and reported success against
#     nothing.)
#   * The route comes from ROUTES below, seventy-four rows, and nothing else; the
#     four claimant rows name the question door family's template, their only
#     door since the old ones were retired (D-7324), refused here, unsent, when a
#     current cap file says the server lacks `questions-v1` (D-7328); `runs
#     review-rounds` is refused the same way for `review-rounds-v1` (D-8125),
#     `goals show` for `goals-read-v1` (D-8126), and `calls record` for `calls-v1`;
#     the seven `plan` rows are refused `no-plan-door` the same way for `plan-v1` (D-8181).
#   * The only caller-supplied text that ever reaches a URL is an id or a query
#     value the row itself declares, each validated against `SAFE_RE` BEFORE the
#     request is built — refused, never escaped and sent.
#
# OUTPUT. stdout is the response body and exactly one newline. stderr ends in
# `http <code>` today; set `CCRC_API_STATUS=line` to keep it once a later release
# stops writing it by default. Exit 0 whenever a response arrived, 3 when none did.
# ONE VERB PRINTS PLAIN TEXT: `mail inbox` (D-6780) prints each unacked mail and
# what it did with it, not a JSON body, writes no status line, and needs `jq`.
#
# `server/test/ccrc-api-closed.test.ts` pins those as standing tests, because a
# property this load-bearing must be a mechanism and not a docstring.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Plenty of routes have no row below, and most of
# them are simply not this client's business. FOUR are absent as a DECISION —
# the release valves D-282 leaves ungated:
#   `POST /api/coord/pause`
#   `POST /api/runs/:id/abandon`
#   `POST /api/claims/:id/break`
#   `POST /api/runs/:id/reclaim`
# They carry no box token on purpose: the party a wedge locks out — the
# coordinator, a session holding a claim, a program whose coordinator is the
# corpse and whose box token died with it — is the party holding that key, so
# putting a wedge's release valve behind it would leave the wedge no door. That
# is precisely why none of them may be reachable from HERE. Coordinator clause 4
# is the other half — "This session never unpauses itself.
# `$REG/coordinator-paused` is the operator's file." — and an ungated door plus a
# session-side verb is a door with no lock at all. They are operator doors,
# reached from the PWA. If the need is ever real it is an operator tool, not a
# row below. (D-688. The names and the count here are not a census anyone keeps
# by hand: `server/test/coord-pause-route.test.ts` derives both from `UNGATED`,
# the one place the set is decided, and reds this passage when they disagree —
# D-1168, which is what this passage cost the last time a docstring stood in for
# a mechanism.)
#
# SELF-CONTAINED. This file `source`s nothing. The moment it does it needs the
# shim treatment `ccrc` has (deploy/deploy.sh argues that at length) and a lone
# copy at ~/.local/bin would be dead on every box. The GENERATED SERVER-CAP
# READER region below is a byte copy of the hook's `_hook_server_cap`, renamed
# `_api_server_cap` (D-7321), never a `source`; regenerate it with
# `ccrc restamp ccd/ccrc-api`.
set +x; set -uo pipefail   # `set +x` FIRST, a SECRETS control as in ccd-pool-sync: an inherited xtrace would print the box token (R16, D-4096)

# `~/.local/bin` is NOT on the `claude-session@` unit PATH (measured 2026-08-26),
# so the corpora invoke this by explicit path. That is a caller's problem, not
# this file's — but it is why nothing here assumes its own directory is on PATH.

SELF="ccrc-api"
AGENT_ENV="$HOME/.ccrc/agent.env"
TOKEN_FILE="$HOME/.cc-secrets/ccrc-mail.token"
REG="$HOME/.cc-sessions"

# The one pattern any caller-supplied text must match before it reaches a URL.
# No dot, so `..` cannot survive it; no slash, no `?`, no `#`, no space. Run ids
# and delivery ids are integers, session ids and project slugs are
# `[A-Za-z0-9_-]+`, so this is not a narrowing of anything real. Widening it is a
# deliberate table change, not a convenience.
SAFE_RE='^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$'

# METHOD|path|needs-id|comma-separated query keys this row accepts (or `-`).
# The four claimant rows' path is the question door family's template
# (`/api/questions/<ask|approval>-{id}/<rule|release>`): from Q3j it was their
# fifth field, sent only while the server advertised `questions-v1` (D-7325), and
# since the old doors were retired it is their path (D-7324). Every row has four.
# Seventy-four rows, and that word is SCANNED: `server/test/ccrc-api.test.ts`
# derives the count from the table below and reds if this line or the bullet at
# the top of this file states a different one. The original rows were chosen on
# 2026-08-26 by measuring the skill corpora; the standing executable-corpus
# parity in `server/test/ccrc-api-closed.test.ts` now fails whenever either skill
# starts calling an operation this table does not declare.
declare -A ROUTES=(
  [runs.list]='GET|/api/runs|no|closed'
  [runs.open]='POST|/api/runs|no|-'
  [runs.dispatch]='POST|/api/runs/{id}/dispatch|yes|-'
  [runs.advance]='POST|/api/runs/{id}/advance|yes|-'
  [runs.close]='POST|/api/runs/{id}/close|yes|-'
  [runs.items]='POST|/api/runs/{id}/items|yes|-'
  # The READ half of the row above, and it exists because the write half was
  # unusable without it: settling needs item IDs, and until this route shipped
  # nothing published them — `runs list` carries only {done,total}. A live
  # coordinator reached the settle call with four titles and no ids, guessed 1,
  # and was refused `unknown-item`.
  [runs.items-list]='GET|/api/runs/{id}/items|yes|-'
  # A session ASKS the operator to end a run it cannot close (D-8076, spec 2026-11-04 §10): box-token gated and attributed,
  # body `{byId, byUuid, why}`. The coordinator is asked first and the answer comes back by mail. It is a request only; the
  # act itself has no row here.
  [runs.end-request]='POST|/api/runs/{id}/end-request|yes|-'
  # Speed/quality signals re-measured off this run's own rows (paired holds,
  # swap count, wave-done mail count, the two signal lines off the last of
  # them) — a measurement, never a dial (D-2842's carry). Read-only, like the
  # row above.
  [runs.signals]='GET|/api/runs/{id}/signals|yes|-'
  # The coordinator's escalation/demotion/manual-override door (routing spec
  # 2026-09-14 §5.3, slice 5) — never `ccd route` directly (coordinator
  # skill clause 1). Body rides `--json`, the same shape every other POST
  # row here uses.
  [runs.route]='POST|/api/runs/{id}/route|yes|-'
  # The coordinator's bounded cap extension (D-8125): when a work run has reached its review-round cap, the run's claiming
  # coordinator asks for REVIEW_ROUND_CAP more rounds, with a reason. The body rides `--json -` as
  # `{"reason":"…"}` alone: the client fills `byId`/`byUuid` from the pane (`derive_identity`) and refuses a body that names
  # either, so the pair cannot be typed by another pane. The operator's removal of the cap has no row here and never will.
  [runs.review-rounds]='POST|/api/runs/{id}/review-rounds|yes|-'
  # `--program` joins through `runs.program`; `--to` and `--program` are
  # MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE — exactly one, both or neither is a 400 (D-2057;
  # routes.ts's own `(to === null) === (program === null)` guard, not
  # this client's). `--all` has no bare-flag form on this client — the generic
  # `--*` arm always consumes a value — so it rides as `--all 1`, exactly as
  # `GET /api/mail?to=`'s existing `all` key always has.
  [mail.list]='GET|/api/mail|no|to,program,all,limit'
  [mail.send]='POST|/api/mail|no|-'
  # `fetch` and `ack` take the DELIVERY id, never the mail row's own id — two
  # separate autoincrement sequences that agree only for a mail sent to one
  # recipient (D-41). This client cannot tell them apart; it is named in the
  # corpora at every call site instead.
  [mail.fetch]='GET|/api/mail/{id}|yes|-'
  [mail.ack]='POST|/api/mail/{id}/ack|yes|-'
  # `mail inbox` (D-6780): the caller's own unacked deliveries, read with the pane's pair in the query, then one
  # `mail.ack` request per printed entry, through the row above. It takes no argument; see `cmd_inbox`.
  [mail.inbox]='GET|/api/mail/inbox|no|-'
  [peers.list]='GET|/api/peers|no|of,project'
  [lifecycle.list]='GET|/api/lifecycle|no|session'
  [claims.list]='GET|/api/claims|no|project,all'
  [claims.take]='POST|/api/claims|no|-'
  [claims.release]='POST|/api/claims/{id}/release|yes|-'
  [ledger.list]='GET|/api/ledger|no|project'
  [ledger.allocate]='POST|/api/ledger/deviations|no|-'
  # The landing line's read (landing order wave 5e, D-7625): one project's entries, flags and order.
  [landing.show]='GET|/api/landing|no|project,pr'
  # The landing line's two attributed writes (landing order wave 5f): the caller's own body, byId/byUuid included, rides
  # unchanged; nothing fills identity here.
  [landing.entries]='POST|/api/landing/entries|no|-'
  [landing.order]='POST|/api/landing/order|no|-'
  # A token-authenticated session may list only its own children's asks. The
  # client derives both identity fields below; `--parent` is accepted only as a
  # compatibility assertion and may never select somebody else.
  [asks.list]='GET|/api/asks|no|parent'
  # ASYMMETRY, deliberate: `list` derives identity because its identity rides the
  # QUERY the client builds, and a caller-selected `parent` there would be the
  # client choosing whose children to read. `answer` and `release` take theirs
  # from the `--json` body, the same shape every other POST row here uses
  # (`mail.send`, `claims.take`, `claims.release`), and the guard is the route's:
  # it re-measures `fromUuid` against the registry and refuses unless the caller
  # IS the ask's parent. Deriving here would read as a security boundary this
  # client cannot be — identity on this fleet is attribution, not authentication,
  # and every `.uuid` is readable by the one UNIX user these sessions share.
  [asks.answer]='POST|/api/questions/ask-{id}/rule|yes|-'
  [asks.release]='POST|/api/questions/ask-{id}/release|yes|-'
  # The durable feed. `--program` filters through `feed_events.runId` and answers
  # one programme's events; without it the read is the whole box's, oldest-first.
  # An event that names no run is programless and appears only unfiltered.
  [feed.list]='GET|/api/feed|no|program,limit'
  # The coordinator's explicit intent declaration (delegation broker wave 3, Task 15). The ONE row whose grammar is not the generic
  # one: `coordinators intent <set|clear> --session <id>`, parsed by `parse_intent` below BEFORE the generic id and flag parser, which
  # then sees no arguments at all. The path id and the body's `byId`/`byUuid` are DERIVED from this pane; `--session` is only an
  # assertion that must equal the derived id, so it can never select another session. The row declares an id because the route
  # template has one, not because a caller supplies it.
  [coordinators.intent]='POST|/api/coordinators/{id}/intent|yes|-'
  # A lease's retain door (delegation broker wave 5, PR8): the lease's parent, or that parent's coordinator, asks the server to keep a
  # delegated worktree. The `{id}` is the lease id (`SAFE_RE`, like every id); the caller sends exactly `{"byId":…,"byUuid":…}` on
  # `--json -`, and nothing here fills identity.
  [leases.retain]='POST|/api/leases/{id}/retain|yes|-'
  # The child's PermissionRequest hook (coordinator-first routing, plan Task 14, spec §6.3, D-5297). `request` sends
  # the canonical request on `--json -` and is answered `201 {id, waitS}`; `ruling` polls one row with the hook's
  # per-request nonce (`SAFE_RE`, like every id); `key` reads the server's public key, which the install spine pins
  # once into `~/.ccrc/approval-signing.pub`. A child's own TOOL CALL to any of them is refused by its PreToolUse
  # deny (`approvals` as this client's first argument); the hook's calls are not tool calls.
  [approvals.request]='POST|/api/approvals|no|-'
  [approvals.ruling]='GET|/api/approvals/{id}/ruling|yes|nonce'
  [approvals.key]='GET|/api/approvals/key|no|-'
  # The CLAIMANT's three rows (plan Task 16, D-5298): rule, hand over, and read one row it holds. `rule` and `release`
  # take the attribution pair in the `--json` body, as `asks answer`/`asks release` do, and the verdict and reason ride
  # there too: this grammar takes no positional word after the id (D-5489). `show` derives identity from the pane, as
  # `asks list` does, so a session reads only a row held for it. A CHILD's own tool call to any `approvals` row is
  # refused by its PreToolUse deny (plan Task 14), before this client runs.
  [approvals.rule]='POST|/api/questions/approval-{id}/rule|yes|-'
  [approvals.release]='POST|/api/questions/approval-{id}/release|yes|-'
  [approvals.show]='GET|/api/approvals/{id}|yes|-'
  # The test box (test box plan TB9, D-7411, D-7554): its status block, and the tools sync the update lane signals
  # once a roll ends (spec §2.10). Both routes take a session OR the box token; neither takes a body or a query, and the
  # sync sends the box only the fleet host's own release and measured versions. The switch has NO row, on purpose:
  # arming remote runs is the operator's act, from the Settings card.
  [testbox.status]='GET|/api/testbox|no|-'
  [testbox.tools-sync]='POST|/api/testbox/tools-sync|no|-'
  # The programme ledgers (plan PL3): the coordinator's side of the Waiting-on-you list and the Goals pin. All nine
  # are box-token gated and attribution-checked, and a coordinator may act only on its own programme's items and pin. The
  # `{id}` of the first, second and last three is the programme's slug (a `SAFE_RE` shape); of the four item-move rows, the
  # item's id. `items list` is a read that names its caller in the query: `byId` and `byUuid` are the caller's own,
  # supplied as flags.
  [items.raise]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/items|yes|-'
  [items.list]='GET|/api/programmes/{id}/items|yes|byId,byUuid'
  [items.confirm]='POST|/api/items/{id}/confirm|yes|-'
  [items.return]='POST|/api/items/{id}/return|yes|-'
  [items.withdraw]='POST|/api/items/{id}/withdraw|yes|-'
  [items.block]='POST|/api/items/{id}/blocking|yes|-'
  [goals.pin]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/spec|yes|-'
  [goals.modify]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/modifications|yes|-'
  [goals.merged]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/modifications/merged|yes|-'
  # The READ of the pin, `doneWhen` and the open items (autonomy-default wave A2, D-8126): a GET by programme slug, answered to the
  # box token or a session alike. Sent unless a CURRENT cap file omits `goals-read-v1` (the gate beside the claimant rows' below).
  [goals.show]='GET|/api/programmes/{id}/goals|yes|-'
  # The coordinator's call record (autonomy-default wave A3, D-8126): a POST by programme slug that the box token, the
  # attribution pair and ownership all gate on the server. Identity is the PANE's: this client splices `byId` and `byUuid` into the
  # body and REFUSES a body that names either, so a caller cannot attribute a call to another coordinator. Sent unless a CURRENT cap
  # file omits `calls-v1` (the gate beside the goals read's, below).
  [calls.record]='POST|/api/coord/programmes/{id}/calls|yes|-'
  # Linear, through the server's proxy and the key ccrc holds for each workspace (D-7669, #1444): sessions never sign
  # in to Linear and never hold a key. Thirteen rows, nine reads and four writes; the SERVER decides each row's class,
  # never the method, which is why `search`, a read, is a POST: its text is free text, and free text never rides a URL
  # (SAFE_RE refuses it as a query value), so it goes in the `--json` body. `search` has its own path so no issue
  # route's `{id}` can match it. The `{id}` on the five issue rows is an issue identifier (`AAA-12`), held to
  # LINEAR_REF_RE below before anything is sent. `workspace` is a declared query key on every row that acts in one
  # workspace, the POSTs included, and never a body field; only `workspaces`, which lists them all, takes none. Every
  # row carries the pane's identity in its QUERY, appended after the declared keys (the Linear block in `main`).
  [linear.workspaces]='GET|/api/linear/proxy/workspaces|no|-'
  [linear.teams]='GET|/api/linear/proxy/teams|no|workspace'
  [linear.issue]='GET|/api/linear/proxy/issues/{id}|yes|workspace'
  [linear.search]='POST|/api/linear/proxy/search|no|workspace'
  [linear.comments]='GET|/api/linear/proxy/issues/{id}/comments|yes|workspace,limit,after'
  [linear.statuses]='GET|/api/linear/proxy/statuses|no|team,workspace'
  [linear.projects]='GET|/api/linear/proxy/projects|no|team,workspace,limit,after'
  [linear.labels]='GET|/api/linear/proxy/labels|no|team,workspace'
  [linear.users]='GET|/api/linear/proxy/users|no|workspace,limit,after'
  [linear.create]='POST|/api/linear/proxy/issues|no|workspace'
  [linear.update]='POST|/api/linear/proxy/issues/{id}|yes|workspace'
  [linear.comment]='POST|/api/linear/proxy/issues/{id}/comments|yes|workspace'
  [linear.link]='POST|/api/linear/proxy/issues/{id}/links|yes|workspace'
  # The linking standard (D-8147): the pane's own project's Linear team key, visibility override and measured repository
  # visibility, and the mode they give. One read, no id and no query key: the project is the caller's registry record's, and the
  # identity rides the query like every Linear row's (the block in `main` that appends it).
  [linking.show]='GET|/api/linking|no|-'
  # The start report (Overlord W1a, spec §3.3.11): the session hook reports one SessionStart of its own session. `{id}` is the
  # session's id; the body (`fromId`, `fromUuid`, `startedUuid`, `reportId`, `source`, `model`, `rowIncarnation`, `reportedAt`) is
  # one JSON report, as the hook builds it or keeps it on one line of its spool.
  [sessions.start]='POST|/api/sessions/{id}/start|yes|-'
  # The card (Overlord W1a, the card piece; spec §3.3.3 C38): a session reads its OWN card, one page per request. `{id}` is the
  # session's id; the declared query keys are the page and, after the first, the card's hash, and the pane's pair (`byId`,
  # `byUuid`) is filled from `derive_identity` after them, never typed (review 2023 F13, the `runs review-rounds` rule of
  # D-8125). `ccrc-api card` (`cmd_card`) reads one page through this row and prints its text; this row is the raw read.
  [sessions.card]='GET|/api/sessions/{id}/card|yes|page,hash'
  # The plan verbs (Linear hierarchy design, D-8181): `{id}` is the programme's slug. Every one but `plan.show` carries the pane's
  # identity in the QUERY (the `linear.*` block's rule, never the body); `plan.show` is a session-or-token read and sends none. All
  # are refused locally `no-plan-door` when a CURRENT cap file omits `plan-v1`. The second operand of a verb (a checkpoint, a
  # ticket) rides the JSON body.
  [plan.put]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/plan|yes|-'
  [plan.show]='GET|/api/programmes/{id}/plan|yes|-'
  [plan.met]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/plan/met|yes|-'
  [plan.ticket-add]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/plan/tickets|yes|-'
  [plan.ticket-move]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/plan/tickets/move|yes|-'
  [plan.epic-drop]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/plan/epics/drop|yes|-'
  [plan.close]='POST|/api/programmes/{id}/plan/close|yes|-'
)

# A Linear issue identifier, the `<REF>` of the five issue rows (D-7669): a team key that starts with a letter, a hyphen,
# then one to nine digits. Every match also matches SAFE_RE; this is the narrower check on those rows, so `AAA12`,
# `AAA-` and `search` are refused here, locally, as `bad-ref` (D-7685), and never sent. The server checks it again. The classes are spelled
# out, never ranges, for the reason `_api_server_cap` gives: under a UTF-8 locale a range may collate non-ASCII letters.
LINEAR_REF_RE='^[ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz][ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789_]*-[0123456789]{1,9}$'

# >>> D-8270 tmux socket lookup (copied byte for byte into each script that cannot source ccd: tmux-socket-census.test.ts)
# THE ONE tmux SOCKET LOOKUP (D-8270, ruling Q8). One shared tmux server, and every tmux call starts with the argv
# `_TMX_ARGV` holds, so moving the fleet to another server is a change of setting. The setting is the first line of
# ~/.ccrc/tmux-socket: absent or empty is tmux's default server and adds no flag; a socket name puts `-L <name>` first;
# anything else asks no tmux at all and answers why with rc 1, never the "can't find session" that reads as gone. A
# file, not a variable: every process on the box reads one answer, and no caller redirects a door with its environment.
# Twin: shared/tmux-target.ts.
# READ ONCE PER PROCESS, AT LOAD, WITH BUILTINS ONLY: the supervise tick's fork budget (tick-diet spec §9) is exactly
# main's. A statement calls `_tmx …`. A capture `$( … )`, a pipeline stage, or a wrapper that execs its argv
# (`_plat_timeout`, `timeout`, `exec`) spreads `"${_TMX_ARGV[@]}" …` itself: bash runs a lone command there without a
# second fork, and a function in its place costs that fork on every call.
_tmx_sock() {   # [home, default $HOME] -> TMX_SOCK (empty: the default server); rc 1 = the setting cannot be used, TMX_WHY says why
  local f="${1-${HOME-}}/.ccrc/tmux-socket" s=""
  TMX_SOCK=""; TMX_WHY=""
  [ -e "$f" ] || [ -L "$f" ] || return 0
  if [ -f "$f" ] && [ -r "$f" ]; then IFS= read -r s < "$f" || :; else TMX_WHY="$f cannot be read"; return 1; fi
  [[ -z "$s" || "$s" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,63}$ ]] \
    || { TMX_WHY=".ccrc/tmux-socket does not hold a socket name (1 to 64 letters, digits, _ and -, not led by -)"; return 1; }
  TMX_SOCK="$s"
}
# shellcheck disable=SC2120  # the home is optional: every caller today asks for the running user's own (D-8270)
_tmx_argv() {   # [home] -> _TMX_ARGV, the argv every fleet tmux call starts with; run once below, again only when asked
  if _tmx_sock "$@"; then _TMX_ARGV=(tmux ${TMX_SOCK:+-L "$TMX_SOCK"})
  else _TMX_ARGV=(sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "$0" >&2; exit 1' "ccrc: tmux was not asked: $TMX_WHY"); fi
}
_tmx_argv
_tmx() { "${_TMX_ARGV[@]}" "$@"; }   # tmux args… -> that call, on the fleet's server (a statement; see above for a capture)
